s are held accountable for demonstrable downstream harms, incentivizing them to integrate robust, transparent monitoring into their own pipelines. If we stifle the deployment of high-entropy, real-world agents, we ensure the 'treacherous turn' remains a theoretical ghost while we lose the race to build reliable systems. The cost of inaction is not just missed economic growth; it is the loss of the only empirical data that can actually inform the safety of future, more powerful systems.",
